[2026-02-04T17:13:52.026Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V9qWSFZ6exRqD96W3e2yBT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"46brooklyn drug price analysis January 2026 methodology pharmaceutical list prices\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Even with a Trump Deal, Drug Prices Increase - Angry Bear\",\"url\":\"https://angrybearblog.com/2026/01/even-with-a-trump-deal-drug-prices-increase\"},{\"title\":\"46brooklyn Research\",\"url\":\"https://www.46brooklyn.com/\"},{\"title\":\"Trump struck deals with 16 drug companies. But they’re still raising prices this year - OPB\",\"url\":\"https://www.opb.org/article/2026/01/16/trump-struck-deals-with-16-drug-companies-they-re-still-raising-prices/\"},{\"title\":\"Many brand-name drug prices are going up, despite Trump administration deals : NPR\",\"url\":\"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/14/nx-s1-5669401/many-brand-name-drug-prices-are-going-up-despite-trump-administration-deals\"},{\"title\":\"Drug Channels: U.S. Brand-Name Drug Prices Fell in 2025 as the Net Pricing Drug Channel Emerges\",\"url\":\"https://www.drugchannels.net/2026/01/us-brand-name-drug-prices-fell-in-2025.html\"},{\"title\":\"Trump struck deals with 16 drug companies. But they're still raising prices this year\",\"url\":\"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/16/nx-s1-5678915/trumprx-pharma-drug-price-deals-list-prices\"},{\"title\":\"Trump struck deals with 16 drug companies. But they're still raising prices this year | NPR & Houston Public Media\",\"url\":\"https://www.houstonpublicmedia.org/npr/2026/01/16/nx-s1-5678915/trump-struck-deals-with-16-drug-companies-but-theyre-still-raising-prices-this-year/\"},{\"title\":\"Brand Drug List Price Change Box Score — 46brooklyn Research\",\"url\":\"https://www.46brooklyn.com/branddrug-boxscore\"},{\"title\":\"There were 977 price changes on brand drugs in January 2025. Here are all the details. — 46brooklyn Research\",\"url\":\"https://www.46brooklyn.com/news/there-were-977-price-changes-on-brand-drugs-in-january-2025-here-are-all-the-details-nc389\"},{\"title\":\"Pharma Companies Raise List Prices, Including 16 That Had Agreements to Lower Prices with Trump Administration: Report | PharmExec\",\"url\":\"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/companies-raise-list-prices-16-agreements-lower-prices-trump-administration-report\"}]\n\nBased on my search, here's what I found about 46brooklyn's drug price analysis for January 2026:\n\n## 46brooklyn January 2026 Drug Price Analysis\n\n### Key Findings\n\nDrug companies, including the 16 that made deals with the Trump administration, raised the prices of 872 brand-name drugs in the first two weeks of 2026, according to a new analysis by 46brooklyn, a drug price research firm.\n\nThe price increases came in at a median of 4% — the same as last year — despite the deals announced with fanfare by the Trump administration.\n\n### Types of Drugs Affected\nThe drugs with price hikes included medicines to treat cancer, heart failure and Type 2 diabetes. The price of some COVID shots also went up.\n\n### Methodology and About 46brooklyn\n46brooklyn Research is an Ohio non-profit corporation whose purpose is to improve the accessibility and usability of U.S. drug pricing data. 46brooklyn takes the myriad drug pricing data sources scattered across the web and stitches them together into data visualizations that can be used by the public to better understand how the drug supply chain functions. 46brooklyn also writes and publishes original research that uses the data within its public data visualizations to shine light on the hidden and complex underbelly of the drug supply chain.\n\nIn their dashboard, they are not permitted to directly publish WAC (Wholesale Acquisition Cost) prices. They can only publish changes. Also, this dashboard only shows changes in brand drug list prices. It doesn't capture the variable, completely hidden, and ever-growing rebates shuffling around in the dark.\n\n### Context on List Prices\nPharmaceutical list prices are the starting point for negotiations with insurers and the middlemen known as pharmacy benefit managers.\n\nBut of course, those are just the list prices of those medicines, and in the U.S. drug companies produce massive rebates and other forms of discounts through the system that makes the net costs of brand medicines much less than the list prices would suggest.\n\n### Specific Company Examples\nPfizer, which reached the first deal with the Trump administration in September, raised the prices of 72 products in January, according to 46brooklyn. They include a 15% increase on the price of its COVID shot.\n\nMerck also raised prices on 18 products, including Isentress for HIV and Belsomra for insomnia, according to 46brooklyn's data.\n\n### Price Decreases\nThere were 18 big price cuts in the first few days of the year, according to 46brooklyn. That includes four drugs that were part of the first round of Medicare drug price negotiation — an initiative launched by the Biden administration as part of the Inflation Reduction Act.\n\nFiasp, an insulin product, got a 75% price cut in the commercial market, according to the 46brooklyn data.\n\n### Data Tools\n46brooklyn's valuable Brand Drug List Price Change Box Score provides insights on week-by-week drugmaker activity. You can access their analysis directly at 46brooklyn.com/branddrug-boxscore.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecff7720-544d-482b-91e9-9eb1a0db0dee","uuid":"7205a4d3-dd11-4074-ac55-5f5a32f82c5a","tool_use_result":{"query":"46brooklyn drug price analysis January 2026 methodology pharmaceutical list prices","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01TMvZQSXUVAkgaxKJu55XLR","content":[{"title":"Even with a Trump Deal, Drug Prices Increase - Angry Bear","url":"https://angrybearblog.com/2026/01/even-with-a-trump-deal-drug-prices-increase"},{"title":"46brooklyn Research","url":"https://www.46brooklyn.com/"},{"title":"Trump struck deals with 16 drug companies. But they’re still raising prices this year - OPB","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2026/01/16/trump-struck-deals-with-16-drug-companies-they-re-still-raising-prices/"},{"title":"Many brand-name drug prices are going up, despite Trump administration deals : NPR","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/14/nx-s1-5669401/many-brand-name-drug-prices-are-going-up-despite-trump-administration-deals"},{"title":"Drug Channels: U.S. Brand-Name Drug Prices Fell in 2025 as the Net Pricing Drug Channel Emerges","url":"https://www.drugchannels.net/2026/01/us-brand-name-drug-prices-fell-in-2025.html"},{"title":"Trump struck deals with 16 drug companies. But they're still raising prices this year","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/16/nx-s1-5678915/trumprx-pharma-drug-price-deals-list-prices"},{"title":"Trump struck deals with 16 drug companies. But they're still raising prices this year | NPR & Houston Public Media","url":"https://www.houstonpublicmedia.org/npr/2026/01/16/nx-s1-5678915/trump-struck-deals-with-16-drug-companies-but-theyre-still-raising-prices-this-year/"},{"title":"Brand Drug List Price Change Box Score — 46brooklyn Research","url":"https://www.46brooklyn.com/branddrug-boxscore"},{"title":"There were 977 price changes on brand drugs in January 2025. Here are all the details. — 46brooklyn Research","url":"https://www.46brooklyn.com/news/there-were-977-price-changes-on-brand-drugs-in-january-2025-here-are-all-the-details-nc389"},{"title":"Pharma Companies Raise List Prices, Including 16 That Had Agreements to Lower Prices with Trump Administration: Report | PharmExec","url":"https://www.pharmexec.com/view/companies-raise-list-prices-16-agreements-lower-prices-trump-administration-report"}]},"Based on my search, here's what I found about 46brooklyn's drug price analysis for January 2026:\n\n## 46brooklyn January 2026 Drug Price Analysis\n\n### Key Findings\n\nDrug companies, including the 16 that made deals with the Trump administration, raised the prices of 872 brand-name drugs in the first two weeks of 2026, according to a new analysis by 46brooklyn, a drug price research firm.\n\nThe price increases came in at a median of 4% — the same as last year — despite the deals announced with fanfare by the Trump administration.\n\n### Types of Drugs Affected\nThe drugs with price hikes included medicines to treat cancer, heart failure and Type 2 diabetes. The price of some COVID shots also went up.\n\n### Methodology and About 46brooklyn\n46brooklyn Research is an Ohio non-profit corporation whose purpose is to improve the accessibility and usability of U.S. drug pricing data. 46brooklyn takes the myriad drug pricing data sources scattered across the web and stitches them together into data visualizations that can be used by the public to better understand how the drug supply chain functions. 46brooklyn also writes and publishes original research that uses the data within its public data visualizations to shine light on the hidden and complex underbelly of the drug supply chain.\n\nIn their dashboard, they are not permitted to directly publish WAC (Wholesale Acquisition Cost) prices. They can only publish changes. Also, this dashboard only shows changes in brand drug list prices. It doesn't capture the variable, completely hidden, and ever-growing rebates shuffling around in the dark.\n\n### Context on List Prices\nPharmaceutical list prices are the starting point for negotiations with insurers and the middlemen known as pharmacy benefit managers.\n\nBut of course, those are just the list prices of those medicines, and in the U.S. drug companies produce massive rebates and other forms of discounts through the system that makes the net costs of brand medicines much less than the list prices would suggest.\n\n### Specific Company Examples\nPfizer, which reached the first deal with the Trump administration in September, raised the prices of 72 products in January, according to 46brooklyn. They include a 15% increase on the price of its COVID shot.\n\nMerck also raised prices on 18 products, including Isentress for HIV and Belsomra for insomnia, according to 46brooklyn's data.\n\n### Price Decreases\nThere were 18 big price cuts in the first few days of the year, according to 46brooklyn. That includes four drugs that were part of the first round of Medicare drug price negotiation — an initiative launched by the Biden administration as part of the Inflation Reduction Act.\n\nFiasp, an insulin product, got a 75% price cut in the commercial market, according to the 46brooklyn data.\n\n### Data Tools\n46brooklyn's valuable Brand Drug List Price Change Box Score provides insights on week-by-week drugmaker activity. You can access their analysis directly at 46brooklyn.com/branddrug-boxscore."],"durationSeconds":23.258972999999997}}
[2026-02-04T17:13:52.501Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MnmqKNq6gZGx6uHVcGrTsk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"pharmaceutical lobbying Trump administration 2025 2026 campaign donations PhRMA\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Pharmaceutical Industry on Pace for Record Lobbying Spending\",\"url\":\"https://readsludge.com/2025/08/29/pharmaceutical-industry-on-pace-for-record-lobbying-spending/\"},{\"title\":\"Pharma PAC That Donated to Trump Breaks With Him Over Executive Order - Newsweek\",\"url\":\"https://www.newsweek.com/pharma-pac-donald-trump-drug-prices-executive-order-2071218\"},{\"title\":\"Big Pharma is Fueling Republicans' Radical MAGA Agenda for 2025\",\"url\":\"https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-features/big-pharma-fueling-maga-agenda-1234953950/\"},{\"title\":\"How Big Pharma Bought Government to Protect Its Racket – CEPR\",\"url\":\"https://cepr.net/publications/how-big-pharma-bought-government-to-protect-its-racket/\"},{\"title\":\"Trump moves against direct-to-consumer drug ads despite massive lobbying by pharmaceutical industry - OpenSecrets News\",\"url\":\"https://www.opensecrets.org/news/2025/10/trump-moves-against-direct-to-consumer-drug-ads-despite-massive-lobbying-by-pharmaceutical-industry\"},{\"title\":\"Big Pharma Lobbying Makes Medicine Far More Expensive\",\"url\":\"https://jacobin.com/2025/11/big-pharma-lobbying-drug-prices\"},{\"title\":\"Pharmaceutical Lobbying In 2025 And US Policy Influence\",\"url\":\"https://dctransparency.com/hidden-power-of-drug-lobbyists-political-influence-and-regulatory-pushback-in-us/\"},{\"title\":\"Pharma Lobby Threads Needle as Trump Agenda Challenges Industry\",\"url\":\"https://news.bloomberglaw.com/health-law-and-business/pharma-lobby-threads-needle-as-trump-agenda-challenges-industry\"},{\"title\":\"Pharmaceutical Research & Manufacturers of America Profile: Summary • OpenSecrets\",\"url\":\"https://www.opensecrets.org/orgs/pharmaceutical-research-manufacturers-of-america/summary?all=2024&id=D000000504\"},{\"title\":\"Trump’s Inauguration Donors: Big Oil, Big Pharma, Big Tech, Crypto, and More\",\"url\":\"https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-features/trump-inauguration-donors-big-oil-big-pharma-big-tech-crypto-1235252214/\"}]\n\nHere are the search results on pharmaceutical lobbying, the Trump administration, campaign donations, and PhRMA in 2025-2026:\n\n## Lobbying Spending\n\nThe pharmaceutical industry spent $227 million on federal lobbying in the first half of 2025, on pace to easily surpass its all-time high of $388 million for 2024. The industry's main trade association, Pharmaceutical Research and Manufacturers of America (PhRMA), is consistently among the top spenders in Washington. In the first quarter of 2025 alone, PhRMA logged its highest-ever quarterly spending at nearly $13 million.\n\nPhRMA spent over $20.6 million on lobbying the federal government in the first half of 2025, including more than $7.6 million in the second quarter.\n\n## Inaugural Donations and Campaign Contributions\n\nPhRMA donated $1 million to Trump's 2025 inaugural fund but did not give to his campaign, according to OpenSecrets. FEC data suggests the group didn't donate to Trump or his Save America PAC in the 2024 election. It did, however, donate to a mix of Democratic and Republican House and Senate candidates.\n\nPhRMA, Pfizer, Bayer, Merck, Eli Lilly, and Abbott Labs each funneled between $500,000 and $1 million to Trump's 2025 inaugural committee.\n\nThe powerful drug lobby PhRMA donated $1 million to Trump's inauguration. So did drugmakers Bayer and Johnson & Johnson. The National Association of Manufacturers, a lobbying group that represents numerous industries, including the pharmaceutical sector, also donated $1 million.\n\n## Dark Money Donations\n\nRecords obtained by political watchdog group Issue One show that PhRMA last year gave millions to a Republican-aligned dark money group. PhRMA shelled out more than $5.6 million in political donations last year, including a $4 million donation to the American Action Network, a dark-money group aligned with House Republican leadership. Between 2020 and 2023, PhRMA funneled $17.5 million to the dark-money group.\n\nPhRMA donated $530,000 to groups involved with the far-right Project 2025 agenda.\n\n## Policy Wins and Lobbying Impact\n\nWeakening drug pricing rules helped turn PhRMA's $4 million dark-money contribution into an $8 billion windfall for drugmakers, according to a Congressional Budget Office estimate. At the behest of the pharmaceutical lobby — and after millions in political donations — Republican lawmakers have defanged rules that would lower the prices of life-saving medicines.\n\nEven as Trump administration threats loomed, the industry scored a major win this year with expanded orphan drug exemptions from Medicare price negotiations, showing that its influence in the Republican-controlled government remains formidable.\n\n## Industry Response to Trump Policies\n\nPhRMA, which donated $1 million to President Donald Trump's inaugural fund, came out against his new executive order aimed at lowering drug prices. When Trump reintroduced in May his \"most favored nation\" policy that seeks to peg US drug prices to cheaper ones paid abroad, PhRMA launched a campaign highlighting why Americans pay more for prescription medicine. Trump \"is right to use trade negotiations to force foreign governments to pay their fair share for medicines,\" but US patients \"should not foot the bill for global innovation.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecff7720-544d-482b-91e9-9eb1a0db0dee","uuid":"3e2413a9-0d3f-4282-b23b-3bc038b47a39","tool_use_result":{"query":"pharmaceutical lobbying Trump administration 2025 2026 campaign donations PhRMA","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu